Online DNS lookup services - Quick and easy DNS lookups


Additionally, we can find the hosts file on our own machines, which allow us to associate an address with an IP address

Looking at DNS records allows us to uncover assets on the target network, map the internal infrastructure through assumptions we make about how the servers communicate. Finally, we can also look for changes in the DNS records.

Common dig Commands
Command	Description
dig domain.com		Performs a default A record lookup for the domain.
dig domain.com A	Retrieves the IPv4 address (A record) associated with the domain.
dig domain.com AAAA	Retrieves the IPv6 address (AAAA record) associated with the domain.
dig domain.com MX	Finds the mail servers (MX records) responsible for the domain.
dig domain.com NS	Identifies the authoritative name servers for the domain.
dig domain.com TXT	Retrieves any TXT records associated with the domain.
dig domain.com CNAME	Retrieves the canonical name (CNAME) record for the domain.
dig domain.com SOA	Retrieves the start of authority (SOA) record for the domain.
dig @1.1.1.1 domain.com	Specifies a specific name server to query; in this case 1.1.1.1
dig +trace domain.com	Shows the full path of DNS resolution.
dig -x 192.168.1.1	Performs a reverse lookup on the IP address 192.168.1.1 to find the associated host name. You may need to specify a name server.
dig +short domain.com	Provides a short, concise answer to the query.
dig +noall +answer domain.com	Displays only the answer section of the query output.
dig domain.com ANY	Retrieves all available DNS records for the domain (Note: Many DNS servers ignore ANY queries to reduce load and prevent abuse, as per RFC 8482).



