DNS zone transfers

while dns brute forcing is typically fruitful, its quieter to look at DNS zone transfers. they exist to replicate DNS records between name servers, and can become a goldmine for information for us. If the zone transfer data is leaked mid transfer, this reveals a detailed map of a server, along with subdomains, IP addresses, and other sensitive DNS data.

vulnerabilities in DNS zone transfers come from misconfigured DNS servers.

in order to test a server for a name transfer vulnerability, we use:
dig axfr @nsztm1.digi.ninja zonetransfer.me

this requests a name transfer for zonetransfer.me




Virtual Hosts
web servers achieve hosting multiple websites on the same machine using virtual hosting, which allows them to differentiate between domains, subdomains, and even seperate websites with distinct content.

The key difference between  Vhosts and Subdomains is their relationship to the DNS and web servers configuration.

Subdomains - an extention of the main name: main.com has subdomain subdomain.main.com
these have their own DNS records

Virtual hosts - configurations within a web server that allow multiple websites or apps to be hosted on a single server. they can be associated with top level domains. each virtual host can have its own separate configuration, enabling precise control over how requests are handled.

there are a few types of virtual hosting, being:

Name-Based Virtual Hosting: This method relies solely on the HTTP host header to distinguish between websites. it is the most common and flexible method. However, it relise on the web server to support name based virtual hosting, and can have limitations with SSL/TLS

IP-Based Virtual Hosting: This assings a unique IP address to each website hosted on a server. generally better, but more expensive and less scaleable

Port-Based Virtual Hosting: DIfferent websites are associated with different ports on the same IP address. this isnt common or user friendly


a few tools can be used to discover vhosts, like: gobuster, Feroxbuster, ffuf


Gobuster
gobuster systematicallt sends HTTP requests with different Host headers to a target IP address and then analyses the responses to identify valid virtual hosts.

There are a couple things to prepare before brute forcing:
Target IP address
Wordlist

the usual command to bruteforce virtualhosts looks like:
gobuster vhost -u http://<ip_address> -w <wordlist_file> --append-domain

other useful arguments worth knowing are:
-t 	set the number of threads for faster scanning
-k	ignore SSL/TLS certificate errors
-o	save output to a file for later analysis



Certificate transparency logs
these are append only ledgers that record the issuance of SSL/TLS certificates. Whenever a certificate authority issues a new certificate, it must submit it to multiple CT logs. independent organisations maintain these logs, and they are open for all to inspect


CT logs are perfect for our purposes, as they typically detail all the subdomains and domains used by a web service, even defunct ones or ones that may not be easily guessable.


one tool we can use is crt.sh, which provides both a website, and an API which allows us to run queries through our CLI.

to interact with the API and get an output in JSON, we can use the following command:

curl -s "https://crt.sh/?
q=<website.com>&output=json" | jq -r '.[]
| select(.name_value | contains("<extra_serach_param>")) | .name_value' | sort -u



FINGERPRINTING

fingerprinting is a cornerstone for web recon for many reasons:

Targeted attacks - knowing what technologies are in use, an attacker can focus their efforts on breaking those systems.
Identifying misconfigurations - Its important for attackers to find and exploit possible open doors
Prioritising targets - This allows attackers to identify vulnerable targets on a network, as well as targets potentially holding the most valuable data
Building a comprehensive profile - This allows attackers to understand security posture and potential attack vectors.

fingerprinting techniques
Banner Grabbing - getting and analysing the banners prevented by web servers and other services, this can reveal server software, version numbers, and other details.
Analysing HTTP headers - HTTP headers transmitted with every web page request and response often offer a wealth of information. The 'server' header typically discloses the web server software, while the 'X-Powered-By' header might reveal additional technologies like scripting languages or frameworks.
Probing for specific requests - Sending specially crafted requests to the target machine can elicit unique responses that reveal specific technologies or versions. For example, certain error messages or behaviours are characteristic of particular web servers or  software components.
Analysing page content - A web page's content, including its structure, scripts, and other elements, can often provide clues about the underlying technologies. There may be a copyright header that indicates specific software being used, for example.

A variety of tools exist that automate the fingerprinting process, combining various techniques to identify web servers, OS's and content management systems, as well as other technologies. some of these tools are:

Wappalyzer	Identifies a wide range of web technologies, incuding CMSs, frameworks, analytics tools, and more.
BuiltWith	Web technology profiler that provides detailed reports on a websites technology stack.
WhatWeb		Uses a vast database of signatures to identify various web technologies
Nmap		Using NSE scripts can perform more sophistocated fingerprinting
Netcraft	provides detailed reports on a website's technology, hosting provider, and security posture
wafw00f		Helps determine if a WAF is present, and its type and configuration


