WEB CRAWLING
crawling - often spidering - is the automated process of systematically browsing the world wide web.
A crawler will be pointed at a webserver, and will start looking through its contents and returning useful info. When it finds a new link in that webpage, it adds it to a queue.

There are two primary types of web crawling, breadth-first and depth-first:
Breadth-first is where a crawler will identify its first layer of targets, probably one site. In that site, it may find links, these links will be the second layer. it goes to the first link in the second layer and finds more links. it will not go to these links (third layer) until it has finished crawling the second layer.
This is useful for getting a broad overview of a websites structure and content

Depth-first is where a crawler will be given a "seed url", this will be its basem and it will gradually work its way deeper and deeper into the web of connected pages.

Extracting valuable information

Links - links to other pages on and off the target site.
comments - comment sections in blogs, forums, or other interactive pages can be a goldmine of information. they can reveal sensitive details, internal processes, or hints of vulnerabilities
Metadata - this is data about data, like page titles, descriptions, keywords, author names, and dates
Sensitive Files - This includes backup files (.bak, .old) config files (web.config, settings.php, *.conf) log files (access_log, error_log, *.log) and other files containing passwords, API keys, or other confidential information, such as database credentials, encryption keys, or even source code snippets.


ROBOTS FILE

this is used to tell web crawlers where they arent allowed to crawl, but they can be set to dissalow access to parts of a site for all user-agents.

some browsers may allow you to manually visit the disallowed pages, its more of a polite request not to browse to these pages, and most legitimate web crawlers will also adhere to these directives.

in recon, robots.txt is used to:
Uncover hidden directories
Map website structure
Detect crawler traps



WELL KNOWN URIs
the .well-known standard, accessible via the /.well-known/ path on a web server centralises a websites critical metadata, including config files and information related to its services, protocols, and security mechanisms

some always presend (R) or optional(O) pages in .well-known are denoted below:

security.txt(R)		contains contact information to report vulnerabilities
change-password(O)	provides a standard URL for users to change their password
openid-configuration(R)	defines config details for OpenID Connect, an identity layer ontop of OAuth 2.0
assetlinks.json(R)	used for verifying ownership of digital assets associated with a domain
mta-sts.txt(R)		SPecifies the polict for SMTP MTA Strict transpot security to enhance email security

the exploration of openid-configuration provides multiple exploration opportunities:
Endpoint discovery - identifying Authorisation endpoints(URL for UAuth requests), Token Endpoints(Finding where URL tokens are issued), and Userinfo Endpoints(the endpoint that provides user information)
JWLS URI -  This reveals the JSON Web Key Set, detailing the cryptographic keys used by the server
Supported scopes and response types - understanding which scopes and response types are supported helps in mapping out the functionality and limitations of the OpenID connect implimentation.
Algorithm Details - information about supported signing alorithms can be crucial for understanding the security measures in place


